\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces The six evaluated methods relative to the exact program. The program (top) defines the objective and constraints but is not solved; each rung approximates it at runtime. M1 applies the adaptive plan alone; M2--M5 each add one priority layer, and the boxes state what the layer adds and how its candidate is committed, which is where the rungs differ: M2 commits the $Q$-argmax under a cost veto, M3 requires the net-benefit and cascade test, M4 maximises the Nash product, and M5 maximises the coalition (team) product. All rungs use the same measured state, guards, and $Z_4$ cap; M0 is the fixed-time reference.}}{48}{figure.caption.121}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:objectives}{{13}{48}{The six evaluated methods relative to the exact program. The program (top) defines the objective and constraints but is not solved; each rung approximates it at runtime. M1 applies the adaptive plan alone; M2--M5 each add one priority layer, and the boxes state what the layer adds and how its candidate is committed, which is where the rungs differ: M2 commits the $Q$-argmax under a cost veto, M3 requires the net-benefit and cascade test, M4 maximises the Nash product, and M5 maximises the coalition (team) product. All rungs use the same measured state, guards, and $Z_4$ cap; M0 is the fixed-time reference}{figure.caption.121}{}}
\newlabel{fig:objectives@cref}{{[figure][13][2147483647]13}{[1][48][]48}}
